WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.717
Consider an agent used by a small business. The agent is asked to
summarize yesterday's failed payments.

00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:26.842
The agent uses external tools such as APIs or MCPs to fetch failed
payments, identify the affected accounts, and retrieve relevant
support tickets. Each result returns to the model before it can choose
the next request. That adds inference work and makes the next request
wait.

00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:34.279
In this illustrative sequence, three requests and a final
interpretation take four model rounds.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:51.012
Instead of sequential tool calling, the model may write a program that
calls the three tools. Inside the program, it totals the failed
payments, identifies the impacted accounts, and links them to three
support tickets - without another model round.

00:00:52.333 --> 00:01:03.044
The program returns the final report. The model provides the requested
summary, financial impact, and customer list while keeping raw records
separate from the primary output.

00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:13.927
The process is reduced from four model rounds to two. Routine work
runs as code, maintaining a smaller, more focused conversation
context.

00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:29.862
Anthropic demonstrated lower context use. CodeAct improved task
success. Cloudflare built a compact interface to thousands of API
endpoints. These results help explain why code execution is becoming
part of the agent stack.

